# Colour bars: guide_colourbar

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`guide_colourbar()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/guide_colourbar.html), which covers
`guide_colorbar()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first
and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is
waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

A colour bar is the legend of a continuous colour or fill scale: a bar shaded through the scale's
colours, with ticks and labels at its breaks. It is chosen in the scale's `guide` argument or in
`guides()`, by name or by calling `guide_colourbar()`, whose options set the number of bins the bar
is shaded in, whether the ticks at its ends are drawn, and, through a theme of its own, the bar's
size and the look and place of its ticks and labels. `guide_colorbar()` is the same function. Giggly
draws a continuous colour's or fill's colour bar as ggplot2 does at its defaults, in its gradient
form (`display = "gradient"`, fifteen colours), but has none of the rest yet: no `guides()`, no
`guide_colourbar()` and its options, and every plot drawn in ggplot2's default theme,
`theme_gray()`, with no way to change it.

## The basic form

`expand.grid()` makes a row for every pair of `X1` and `X2` from 1 to 10, and `value` is their
product; `p2` adds points sized by `value`, for the last example. The plots are the same: a
colour bar is the default guide for a continuous fill, chosen in the scale, by name or by
function, or in `guides()`.

```r
df <- expand.grid(X1 = 1:10, X2 = 1:10)
df$value <- df$X1 * df$X2

p1 <- ggplot(df, aes(X1, X2)) + geom_tile(aes(fill = value))
p2 <- p1 + geom_point(aes(size = value))

# Basic form
p1 + scale_fill_continuous(guide = "colourbar")
p1 + scale_fill_continuous(guide = guide_colourbar())
p1 + guides(fill = guide_colourbar())
```

Not in Giggly yet: tiles (`geom_tile()`), `scale_fill_continuous()` and its `guide` option,
`guides()` and `guide_colourbar()`.

## Styling the bar

A guide's `theme` styles only that guide: here the bar's width and height, no labels, no ticks,
the labels on the left, and blue labels.

```r
# Control styles

# bar size
p1 + guides(fill = guide_colourbar(theme = theme(
  legend.key.width  = unit(0.5, "lines"),
  legend.key.height = unit(10, "lines")
)))

# no label
p1 + guides(fill = guide_colourbar(theme = theme(
  legend.text = element_blank()
)))

# no tick marks
p1 + guides(fill = guide_colourbar(theme = theme(
  legend.ticks = element_blank()
)))

# label position
p1 + guides(fill = guide_colourbar(theme = theme(
  legend.text.position = "left"
)))

# label theme
p1 + guides(fill = guide_colourbar(theme = theme(
  legend.text = element_text(colour = "blue", angle = 0)
)))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_tile()`, `guides()`, `guide_colourbar()` and its `theme` option, and
changing the theme.

## The number of bins

```r
# small number of bins
p1 + guides(fill = guide_colourbar(nbin = 3))

# large number of bins
p1 + guides(fill = guide_colourbar(nbin = 100))
```

`nbin` sets how many steps the bar's shading is drawn in.

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_tile()`, `guides()`, and `guide_colourbar()` and its `nbin` option.

## Hiding the end ticks

```r
# make top- and bottom-most ticks invisible
p1 +
  scale_fill_continuous(
    limits = c(0,20), breaks = c(0, 5, 10, 15, 20),
    guide = guide_colourbar(nbin = 100, draw.ulim = FALSE, draw.llim = FALSE)
  )
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_tile()`, `scale_fill_continuous()` and its `limits`, `breaks` and `guide`
options, and `guide_colourbar()` and its `nbin`, `draw.ulim` and `draw.llim` options.

## A colour bar beside a legend

`p2` has a colour bar for fill and a legend of sizes. Each scale's guide is chosen on its own, in
the scale or in `guides()`; in the last plot, each guide also takes its own direction.

```r
# guides can be controlled independently
p2 +
  scale_fill_continuous(guide = "colourbar") +
  scale_size(guide = "legend")
p2 + guides(fill = "colourbar", size = "legend")

p2 +
  scale_fill_continuous(guide = guide_colourbar(theme = theme(
    legend.direction = "horizontal"
  ))) +
  scale_size(guide = guide_legend(theme = theme(
    legend.direction = "vertical"
  )))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_tile()`, mapping `size`, `scale_fill_continuous()`, `scale_size()`,
`guides()`, `guide_colourbar()` and `guide_legend()` and their `theme` options, and changing the
theme.